 6. 🗣️ Orators&#x27; new age 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Maura Losch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 "As reading declines , ideas are spreading not by text but by spoken word" — a lasting piece of Charlie Kirk's legacy, The Economist reports ($): 



 "Ideas that were once disseminated by headlines and columns are now being conveyed by TikToks and podcasts. As people spend more time absorbed in their screens and their AirPods, the human voice is making a comeback. That has consequences for how people communicate — and perhaps for the kinds of ideas that spread." 🤔 The intrigue: As oration comes back into vogue, so do its quirks. Written arguments are typically clean and structured, while spoken ones can repeat and ramble as people work toward their conclusion in real time.

 "The oral turn also helps explain the success of ... Donald Trump," The Economist notes. "Some have even compared his 'tagging' of enemies — 'crooked Hillary,' 'sleepy Joe' and so on — to Homer, who used epithets like 'ox-eyed Hera' and 'crooked-counseled' Cronos to lodge characters in audiences' minds." 
 
